    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d460">

                <pb facs="0458" n="446" sameAs="frus:frus1939v01/pg446"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.0011 European War 1939/473: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the Soviet Union</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Steinhardt</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Moscow</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1939-09-21T18:00:00+03:00">September 21,
                            1939—6 p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received September 21—5:06 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>585. My telegram No. 557, September 17, 9 p.m. I am informed by a member of the
                    French Embassy that the French Government has decided not to break off relations
                    with the Soviet Government by reason of the Soviet invasion of Poland and that
                    up to the present time the French Embassy here has made no formal protest to the
                    Soviet Government on the matter. I understand that the decision of the British
                    Government will be similar to that of the French. The French Chargé d’Affaires
                    yesterday saw Potemkin<anchor n="71" xml:id="d460-f1"/> and requested an oral
                    explanation of the arbitrary action against Poland.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Steinhardt</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d460-f1" n="71" xml:id="d460-f1-content">
                <p>Vladimir P. Potemkin, Soviet First Assistant Commissar for Foreign Affairs.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
